%[text] ## Exercise 2: Finding the full width at half maximum of a spectral peak 
%[text] A detector scan can be represented by a measured irradiance as a function of position; if the light is dispersed by a prism or grating, that distance maps to a wavelength.   Here the signal is a Lorentzian where $I\_0$ is the peak intensity, $\\lambda$ is the wavelength,  $\\lambda\_0$ is the central wavelength, and $\\Delta\\lambda$ is the spectral width (full width at half maximum).  
%[text]{"align":"center"} $I(\\lambda\n)= \\frac{I\_0}{\\left(1 + 4\\frac{(\\lambda-\\lambda\_0)^2}{\\Delta\\lambda^2}\\right)}.$
%[text] Tasks:
%[text] 1\. Define the parameters:  Use a central wavelength of 1064 nm, a spectral width of 10 nm, and a  
%[text] 2\. Define an array of wavelengths centered around the central wavelength extending over at least 10 spectral widths (5 on each side).   
%[text] 3\. Calculate and plot the irradiance as a function of wavelength
%[text] 4\. Use a logical variable to find the points closest to the half power point.  Hint:  The function $\\left(I(\\lambda) - \\frac{I\_0}{2}\\right)^2$ will be at a minimum at the half power point.  Add these points to the plot. 
%[text] 5\.  The difference between these values is the full width at half maximum 
%[text] 
%[text] ## Exercise 2 Work Area
%[text] TODO: Add your code for Exercise 2 here.

% Define constants (central wavelength, spectral width and peak intensity)
lambda0 = 1064 ;
delta_lambda = 10 ; 
I_0 = 1; 
% Define an array for the wavelength 
wls = linspace(-5*delta_lambda, 5*delta_lambda,201)+lambda0; 
% Calculate the irradiance
Irr = I_0./(1+4*((wls-lambda0).^2./(delta_lambda)^2));
% Find the wavelengths closest to the half power point
ftest = abs(Irr-I_0/2) ; 
[fmin,ll] = min(ftest);
w1 = wls(ll);
% Check and see if it found two minima or one
if length(w1)==2
    w1 = w1(1);
    w2 = w1(2);
else
    w2 = lambda0 - (w1 - lambda0);
end
    FWHM = abs(w1-w2);
% Display the full width at half maximum

display(['Full Width at Half Maximum:' num2str(FWHM) ' nm']) %[output:935ac4f5]

figure('Name','Solution 2: Spectral Peak') %[output:00649dd8]
plot(wls,Irr,'LineWidth',1.3); hold on %[output:00649dd8]
plot(w1,Irr(ll),'ob') %[output:00649dd8]
plot(w2,Irr(ll),'ob') %[output:00649dd8]
grid on %[output:00649dd8]
xlabel('x(\mu m)') %[output:00649dd8]
ylabel('E(x)') %[output:00649dd8]
title(['Spectral peak: FWHM = ' num2str(FWHM,2) ' nm'] ); %[output:00649dd8]

